Description: pvr: discard transform feedback primitives that do not fit
 On the BXM-4-64 the stream-out STM does not honour the limit in SRC3:
 with a bound range smaller than the draw it keeps writing
 behind the end of the range, advances the address and counts the primitive
 in the low word of its SRC1 persistent temp. The counter buffer value and
 the transform feedback stream query are derived from that word, so both
 report primitives that were never meant to be captured.
 .
 Check the space in the stream-out PDS program, which the TA runs once per
 primitive. For every buffer group, the end of the primitive (address in
 SRC0 bits 39..0 plus the bytes up to the end of its last vertex) is compared
 against the limit (SRC3 bits 39..0), and if it does not fit, a branch skips
 the STMs of all groups. A primitive is thus written to all buffers or to
 none, as Vulkan and GL require, and the STM counter of every group only
 counts primitives that were written. The written count is therefore the
 same for every buffer, which makes the minimum over the buffers (separate
 attribs) equal to the count of group 0 that the query already reads. The
 per group size is computed on the CPU from the varyings of the group:
 (vertices per primitive - 1) * stride + the end of the last captured
 component. No division is needed and no further persistent temp is used.
 .
 The primitives generated count of VK_EXT_primitives_generated_query stays
 behind the branch target, so it still counts every primitive.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
